  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 200 AM Friday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... The persistent pattern of dry and seasonable \nweather will continue over the next 7 days.\n\nThe mid and upper level weather pattern through the weekend will be \ndominated by a high amplitude upper level ridge extending from the \nwestern Gulf north into the western Great Lakes with a general \nnortherly flow across the Carolinas. Disturbances in the flow \nincluding a series of moisture-starved shortwave troughs will move \nacross the Carolinas tonight and early Sunday. Deep layer moisture \nwill be lacking as precipitable water values will remain low through \nthe period ranging between 30-60% of normal. \n\nA reinforcing surface high pressure system will progress east from \nthe Great Lakes into the the Canadian Maritimes over the next few \ndays. This high will extend into our region behind a dry cold front \nthat will move across the Carolinas this morning. A slightly cooler \nairmass will arrive today and a northeasterly flow will spread \nshallow moisture and generate some fair weather stratocumulus clouds \nacross the area during the today and Saturday, especially eastern \nareas. These will be the first notable clouds in central NC since \nMonday. Temperatures will generally range near and slightly below \naverage through the weekend. \n\nThe mid and upper level ridge will begin to weaken early next week \nwith another short wave trough moving across the region during the \nmiddle of the week. The airmass will moderate on Monday and Tuesday \nwith temperatures warming up a few degrees before a slightly cooler \nairmass arrives for Wednesday an Thursday. Dry weather should \ncontinue for through Thursday although there are signals that a more \nsouthwesterly flow will result in an increase in deep layer moisture \nand perhaps the need to consider limited rain chances next weekend.",
